    "q-103384": {
      "dom_id": "step-q103384",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $20≡13\\,(\\text{mod}\\,5)$\n\nII. $20≡14\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3)$\n\nIII. $15≡1\\,(\\text{mod}\\,7)$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we recall that \n\n$$\na≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,n∣(a-b).\n$$\n\nSo, $a$ and $b$ are congruent modulo $n$ if and only if $(a-b)$ is divisible by $n$ (there is no remainder).\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Case I gives a false statement because $5∤(20-13).$ Notice that $20-13=7,$ which is not divisible by $5.$\n\n- Case II gives a true statement. Indeed, $3∣(20-14).$ There is no remainder:\n\n$$\n(20-14)÷3=6÷3=2\n$$\n\n- Case III gives a true statement. Indeed, $7∣(15-1).$ There is no remainder:\n\n$$\n(15-1)÷7=14÷7=2\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
