    "q-103388": {
      "dom_id": "step-q103388",
      "name": "Question 15",
      "problem": "Given that $9≡23\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n),$ which of the following could be the value of $n?$\n\nI. $n=2$\n\nII. $n=3$\n\nIII. $n=7$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we recall that \n\n$$\na≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,n∣(a-b).\n$$\n\nSo, $a$ and $b$ are congruent modulo $n$ if and only if $(a-b)$ is divisible by $n$ (there is no remainder).\n\nWe have $a-b=9-23=-14.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Note that $2∣(-14)$ because $(-14)÷2=-7,$ and there is no remainder. Therefore, the statement is true when $n=2.$\n\n- Note that $3∤(-14).$ Therefore, the statement is false when $n=3.$\n\n- Note that $7∣(-14)$ because $(-14)÷7=-2,$ and there is no remainder. Therefore, the statement is true when $n=7.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
